Each family hides the same skeleton underneath: name the unknowns, turn
each statement into an equation or fact, and count. The families differ
only in how the unknowns are dressed — as years, rupees, kilometres, or
marks.

## 2. The Equation Count Rule

[definition]
The **equation count rule**: to determine *n* unknowns uniquely you need
*n* independent equations, and a single statement may carry one, two, or
zero of them. Sufficiency is a property of the count, not of the
statement's length.
[/definition]

**Example 1 — both statements needed.** What is the value of x?
- **I.** 3x + 2y = 24
- **II.** y = 3

I alone: one equation, two unknowns — structurally insufficient. II
alone: fixes y but never touches x. Together: 3x + 6 = 24, so x = 6.
The verdict is **both together** — I contributed one equation, II the
second.

**Example 2 — the quadratic trap.** What is the value of x?
- **I.** 2x + 3 = 17
- **II.** x² = 49

I alone: one linear equation, one unknown — x = 7, unique, sufficient.
II alone: x = 7 or x = −7 — *two* values survive, so II is insufficient.
A statement can look like an equation and still fail the uniqueness
test.

[warning]
Squares and square roots are the classic uniqueness killers. "x² = 36"
leaves x = 6 and x = −6 alive; "x is positive and x² = 36" kills one
branch and becomes sufficient. Always ask how many values survive.
[/warning]

**Example 3 — one statement can carry two equations.** What is the value of x?
- **I.** x + y = 12 and x − y = 4
- **II.** xy = 32

Statement I packs *two* independent equations into a single statement —
x = 8 immediately, so I alone is sufficient. II alone (one equation, two
unknowns) is insufficient. The verdict is **I alone**, and the lesson is
that "two statements" never means "two equations".

@diagram flowchart id="equation-count-flow" layout="hierarchical" aria="Equation counting decision flow for arithmetic data sufficiency"
node start label="List the unknowns in the stem" shape="pill"
node s1 label="Does I alone give enough independent equations?" shape="diamond"
node s2 label="Does II alone give enough independent equations?" shape="diamond"
node comb label="Do I and II together give enough?" shape="diamond"
node a label="I alone is sufficient" shape="round-rect"
node b label="II alone is sufficient" shape="round-rect"
node d label="Both together are needed" shape="round-rect"
node e label="Neither is sufficient" shape="round-rect"

start -> s1
s1 -> a
s1 -> s2
s2 -> b
s2 -> comb
comb -> d
comb -> e
@/diagram

[tip]
Run the flowchart mentally, top to bottom, in statement order. If I
alone already gives one clean equation per unknown, stop — the answer is
I alone or both, and you only need II to see which.
[/tip]

## 3. Ages

Age questions convert to linear equations in "present age" unknowns.
Translate phrases first: "five years ago" subtracts 5, "six years hence"
adds 6.

**Example 1 — both needed.** What is Ravi's present age?
- **I.** Ravi is 12 years older than Meena.
- **II.** Five years ago, Meena was half of Ravi's age.

I alone: R = M + 12 — one equation, two unknowns. II alone:
M − 5 = (R − 5)/2 — again one equation, two unknowns. Together:
R = 2M − 5 from II, so M + 12 = 2M − 5, giving M = 17 and R = 29.
Check: five years ago 12 and 24 — exactly half. Verdict: **both
together**.

**Example 2 — either alone.** What is the son's present age?
- **I.** The son's age is one-third of his father's, and the father is 45.
- **II.** Five years ago the son was one-fourth of his father's age, and the father is 45.

I alone: S = 45/3 = 15 — sufficient. II alone: S − 5 = 40/4 = 10, so
S = 15 — sufficient. Two statements, two complete routes to the same
age: **either alone**. When both routes agree like this, "either" is
confirmed.

**Example 3 — I alone.** What is Ashok's present age?
- **I.** Ashok's age is 25% more than Meena's, and Meena is 20 years old.
- **II.** Five years ago Ashok was twice as old as Meena.

I alone: A = 1.25 × 20 = 25 — sufficient. II alone:
A − 5 = 2(M − 5) — one equation, two unknowns. Verdict: **I alone**;
II is a relation, not a value.

## 4. Percentages

Percentage statements are equations in disguise — but only if the *base*
is present. A bare "increased by 10%" determines nothing.

**Example 1 — both needed.** What was the town's population in 2020?
- **I.** The population rose by 10% from 2019 to 2020.
- **II.** The rise amounted to 2,200 people.

I alone: a rate with no base. II alone: an amount with no rate. Together:
10% of the 2019 population is 2,200, so 2019 held 22,000 and 2020 holds
24,200. Verdict: **both together** — rate and base reunite.

**Example 2 — either alone.** What is the value of x?
- **I.** 60% of x is 288.
- **II.** x is three-fourths of 640.

I alone: x = 288/0.6 = 480 — sufficient. II alone: x = (3/4) × 640 =
480 — sufficient. Verdict: **either alone**.

[note]
Percentage DS turns on the phrase "of what?". "10% more" with no noun,
or "20% profit" with no cost price, is an equation missing its base —
hunt the base in the other statement before concluding "neither".
[/note]

## 5. Ratio and Proportion

A ratio is a *shape*, not a size. It suffices only when some absolute
total or difference accompanies it.

**Example 1 — both needed.** How many marbles does Anu have?
- **I.** Anu and Bala together have 84 marbles.
- **II.** Their marbles are in the ratio 5 : 7.

I alone: a total with no split. II alone: a split with no total — 5k and
7k for any k. Together: Anu has 5/12 of 84 = 35. Verdict: **both
together**.

**Example 2 — I alone.** How many red balls are in the bag?
- **I.** The bag holds 60 balls in all, and red : blue is 2 : 3.
- **II.** The blue balls are 50% more numerous than the red balls.

I alone: total plus ratio fixes the parts — red = (2/5) × 60 = 24.
Sufficient. II alone: B = 1.5R is one equation in two unknowns.
Verdict: **I alone**. (With red = 24, blue = 36, and indeed 36 = 1.5 ×
24 — the statements agree, as they must.)

[tip]
Ratio + total is the highest-value pairing in this topic: 5 : 7 with a
sum of 84 dissolves in one line, 5k + 7k = 12k = 84. When you see a bare
ratio in statement I, scan statement II for a total, a difference, or an
absolute number — any one of them completes the size.
[/tip]

## 6. Profit and Loss

Profit questions hide two unknowns (cost price and selling price) and
two bridges between them (profit amount, profit percentage, markup,
discount).

**Example 1 — either alone.** What was the selling price?
- **I.** The discount given was Rs 100, which was 10% of the marked price.
- **II.** The profit was 20% on a cost price of Rs 750.

I alone: marked price = 100 × 10 = 1,000, so selling price = 1,000 −
100 = Rs 900 — sufficient. II alone: 750 × 1.2 = Rs 900 — sufficient.
Verdict: **either alone**.

**Example 2 — both needed.** What is the profit percentage?
- **I.** The article was bought for Rs 400.
- **II.** It was sold for Rs 460.

A percentage of profit needs both prices; each statement supplies one.
Together: profit = 60 on 400, which is 15%. Verdict: **both together**.

[warning]
"Profit percentage" is always a fraction of the *cost* price unless the
stem says otherwise. Candidates who divide by the selling price answer
a question the paper never asked — and mark "sufficient" for the wrong
reason.
[/warning]

## 7. Time, Speed and Distance

Every TSD statement is one equation of the form distance = speed ×
time. Three quantities, two given, one asked — the third is the unknown
you must corner.

**Example 1 — both needed.** What is the speed of the train?
- **I.** It crosses a pole in 12 seconds.
- **II.** It crosses a 240-metre platform in 24 seconds.

I alone: v = L/12 — the length L is a second unknown. II alone:
(L + 240)/24 = v — one equation, two unknowns. Together: L = 12v, so
(12v + 240)/24 = v, giving 12v = 240 and v = 20 m/s. Verdict: **both
together**. Crossing a pole gives length, crossing a platform gives
length + platform — the pair is designed to interlock.

**Example 2 — either alone.** What is the distance between A and B?
- **I.** A car covers it in 4 hours at 45 km/h.
- **II.** A bus covers it in 3 hours at 60 km/h.

I alone: 4 × 45 = 180 km — sufficient. II alone: 3 × 60 = 180 km —
sufficient. Verdict: **either alone**.

**Example 3 — I alone.** What is the speed of the bus?
- **I.** The bus covers 150 km in 2.5 hours.
- **II.** The bus takes 2 hours more than a car moving at 80 km/h for the same trip.

I alone: 150/2.5 = 60 km/h — sufficient. II alone: the bus's time is
tied to an unknown distance — one equation, two unknowns (bus speed and
distance). Verdict: **I alone**.

## 8. Traps and Timing

| Trap | Signature | Defence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| One equation, two unknowns | "x is twice y" with no absolute number | count unknowns before touching algebra |
| Quadratic doubles the answers | x² = 49, or area/perimeter pairs | demand a single surviving value |
| Percentage without base | "increased 10%" and no reference number | hunt the base in statement II |
| Ratio without total | 5 : 7 and no sum or difference | hunt a total, difference, or absolute part |
| Unit drift | seconds vs hours, m/s vs km/h, paise vs rupees | convert once, early, in the margin |
| Partial data read as full | "sold at 15% profit" without cost or price | name what is missing before judging |

[tip]
Budget **30–45 seconds per question**. The counting habit makes this
realistic: five seconds to name the unknowns, ten per statement to
translate and count, ten to combine or mark. Topics like ages and
percentages reward a second of pattern recognition — "relation only" is
almost never sufficient by itself.
[/tip]

[warning]
Two insufficient statements are not automatically sufficient together.
"I. 3x + 2y = 24. II. x and y are positive integers." — the second adds
no equation, so x stays free. Combining only helps when the second
statement brings an *independent* constraint.
[/warning]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Topic | What suffices | Typical verdict pattern |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ages | relations plus one absolute anchor | both together |
| Percentages | rate and base reunited | either or both |
| Ratio-proportion | ratio plus a total or difference | both or I alone |
| Profit-loss | any two of cost, price, profit %, profit | either or both |
| Time-speed-distance | two of distance, speed, time per object | either or both |
| Simple equations | independent equations equal unknowns | all verdicts occur |

| Point | One-line recap |
| --- | --- |
| Master rule | n unknowns need n independent equations |
| Statement ≠ equation | one statement may hold two equations or zero |
| Uniqueness | x² = 49 fails; x = 7 or "x is positive" rescues it |
| Percentage | no base, no verdict |
| Ratio | no total, no verdict |
| Time budget | 30–45 seconds per question |

[summary]
Arithmetic DS is bookkeeping for equations: name the unknowns, convert
each statement into constraints, and compare counts. Ages want an
anchor, percentages want a base, ratios want a total, profit wants two
prices, and speed wants two of its three quantities — count first, and
never solve further than the verdict requires.
[/summary]
